===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|gros-grain||coarse grain, a strong fabric}}, from {{m|fr|gros|t=coarse}} + {{m|fr|grain|t=grain}}. {{doublet|en|grosgrain}}, which was borrowed later.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɡɹɒ.ɡɹəm/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|LL-Q1860 (eng)-I learned some phrases-grogram.wav|Audio (UK)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A strong [[fabric]]; a mixture of [[silk]] and [[wool]] or [[mohair]].
#* '''1605''', Ben Jonson, George Chapman, and John Marston, ''Eastward Hoe'' Act 1 Scene 2:
#*: I like some humors of the Cittie dames well: to eate cherries onely at an angell a pound, good; to dye rich scarlet black, pretty; to line a '''grogaram''' gowne cleane thorough with velvet, tollerable; their pure linnen, their smocks of 3. li. a smock, are to be borne withall. But your minsing niceryes, taffata pipkins, durance petticotes and silver bodkins—Gods my life, as I shall be a lady, I cannot indure it!
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|title=Women of the Mito Domain: Recollections of Samurai Family Life|author=Kikue Yamakawa, ‎Kate Wildman Nakai|year=2001|passage=They had known nothing of woolen cloth, but now the popularity of obi made of imported '''grogram''' spread like wildfire. This popularity produced various stories in its wake.}}

